To change the text color in a C++ console application, you can use platform-specific libraries and system calls. Below, I'll provide a solution for Windows and a cross-platform solution using ANSI escape codes for Linux/Unix.

## Windows Solution

For Windows, you can use the `SetConsoleTextAttribute` function provided by the Windows API. Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <windows.h>

// Function to set console text color
void setConsoleTextColor(int color) {
    // Get the handle to the standard output
    HANDLE hConsole = GetStdHandle(STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);

    // Set the text color
    SetConsoleTextAttribute(hConsole, color);
}

int main() {
    // Change text color to green
    setConsoleTextColor(2); // Green color
    std::cout << "This is green text!" << std::endl;

    // Change text color to red
    setConsoleTextColor(4); // Red color
    std::cout << "This is red text!" << std::endl;

    // Reset to default text color
    setConsoleTextColor(7); // Default color
    std::cout << "This is default text color." << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the `setConsoleTextColor` function changes the console text color based on the argument passed to it. Various color codes can be used as arguments:

- `0` - Black
- `1` - Blue
- `2` - Green
- `3` - Aqua
- `4` - Red
- `5` - Purple
- `6` - Yellow
- `7` - White
- `8` - Gray
- `9` - Light Blue
- `10` - Light Green
- `11` - Light Aqua
- `12` - Light Red
- `13` - Light Purple
- `14` - Light Yellow
- `15` - Bright White

## Cross-Platform Solution (ANSI Escape Codes)

For a cross-platform solution using ANSI escape codes, which will work on most Unix-like systems including Linux and macOS, you can use the following code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void setConsoleTextColor(int color) {
    // ANSI escape codes for colors
    std::cout << "\033[" << color << "m";
}

int main() {
    // Change text color to green
    setConsoleTextColor(32); // Green color
    std::cout << "This is green text!" << std::endl;

    // Change text color to red
    setConsoleTextColor(31); // Red color
    std::cout << "This is red text!" << std::endl;

    // Reset to default text color
    setConsoleTextColor(0); // Default color
    std::cout << "This is default text color." << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the `setConsoleTextColor` function sends ANSI escape codes to change the text color. The color codes used are:

- `30` - Black
- `31` - Red
- `32` - Green
- `33` - Yellow
- `34` - Blue
- `35` - Magenta
- `36` - Cyan
- `37` - White

To reset to the default text color, you can use `\033[0m`.

Choose the appropriate method based on your target platform. For a cross-platform solution, using ANSI escape codes is usually the best approach.

